--TEST--
`Core\Cli\Text::text` reads back the text the constructor already neutralized, so a rewritten carrier cannot re-inject what was substituted — `rule:tooling/terminal-output-is-a-sink`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 1's substitution happens in the constructor, so what `text` answers is the
// visible glyph and not the control byte it replaced. Reading a carrier
// therefore cannot hand a program back an escape sequence to re-emit.
var $raw = "title" . Core\Str::fromCodePoint(27) . "]0;owned";
var $carrier = Core\Cli\Text::plain($raw);
echo $carrier->text(), "\n";

// And the answer goes back through a constructor unchanged: the second pass has
// nothing left to substitute, which is what makes reading safe on this sink
// where an HTML one would escape the same bytes twice.
var $again = Core\Cli\Text::plain($carrier->text());
echo $again->text(), "\n";

// The carrier still writes raw, so what a reader sees is what the read answered.
echo $again, "\n";
--EXPECT--
title␛]0;owned
title␛]0;owned
title␛]0;owned
